    "final_answer": "Final Answer:\n- Report: outputs/reports/tissue_prediction_report.md\n- Key Results:\n  - Predicted tissue of origin for the unknown RNA-seq sample: **Prostate** (exactly as in the metadata `tissue` column).\n- Artifacts:\n  - Predicted tissue (single-line text): outputs/reports/predicted_tissue.txt\n  - Prediction summary (markdown): outputs/reports/prediction_summary.md\n  - Tissue-level similarity table: outputs/tables/tissue_level_similarity.tsv\n  - Sample-level similarity scores: outputs/tables/sample_similarity_scores.tsv\n  - Normalized reference expression: outputs/tables/reference_expression_normalized.tsv\n  - Normalized unknown expression: outputs/tables/unknown_expression_normalized.tsv\n  - Normalization summary: outputs/tables/normalization_summary.tsv\n  - Harmonized reference counts: outputs/tables/reference_expression_harmonized.tsv\n  - Harmonized unknown counts: outputs/tables/unknown_expression_harmonized.tsv\n  - Shared genes list: outputs/tables/shared_genes.tsv\n- Figures:\n  ![Top tissues by mean Pearson similarity](outputs/figures/tissue_similarity_barplot.png)\n- Caveats & Warnings:\n  - A simple CPM + log2(CPM+1) normalization was used without more advanced methods (e.g., TMM, DESeq2 size factors) or batch correction; results rely on the assumption that remaining technical variation does not dominate tissue-level differences.\n  - The classification is entirely dependent on the provided 2,555-sample reference panel; misannotations or underrepresented tissues in that panel could bias the prediction.\n  - Only one unknown sample was classified, so confidence is based on relative similarity margins (Prostate vs. next-best tissues) rather than a measured error rate on multiple test samples.\n- Next Steps:\n  - If possible, validate the Prostate assignment using orthogonal information (e.g., known prostate marker expression or external metadata).\n  - Inspect expression of canonical prostate markers in the unknown sample versus top-matching and non-matching tissues for qualitative confirmation.\n  - Apply the same pipeline to additional unknown samples, if available, to assess robustness and consistency of tissue predictions.",
